sections.s2_technical:
The gateway is the single entry point, so clients talk to one address instead of knowing every service's location. This decouples clients from the internal service topology, letting services move, split, or scale without breaking clients.

Authentication and authorization happen at the gateway so each service does not re-implement them. The gateway validates a token, such as a JWT, confirms the caller's identity, and can enforce coarse permissions before forwarding, rejecting unauthenticated requests early.

```java
// Gateway authenticates once, then forwards with verified identity
public Response handle(Request req) {
    if (!auth.validate(req.token())) return Response.unauthorized();
    String userId = auth.subject(req.token());
    req.addHeader("X-User-Id", userId);   // pass verified identity downstream
    return route(req);
}
```

Rate limiting protects downstream services from abuse and overload, applied per client or per API key, returning HTTP 429 when exceeded. Routing matches the request path or host to the correct backend service, and load balancing spreads requests across that service's instances, skipping unhealthy ones. TLS termination means the gateway handles encryption with the client, so internal traffic can use simpler or mutually authenticated channels, centralizing certificate management.

```java
// Path-based routing to the right microservice pool
public String route(Request req) {
    if (req.path().startsWith("/api/transfers")) return "transfer-service";
    if (req.path().startsWith("/api/accounts")) return "account-service";
    return "default-service";
}
```

Request and response transformation lets the gateway adapt between the external API and internal formats: aggregating several service calls into one response, stripping internal fields, adding headers, or versioning. A senior nuance is to keep the gateway thin and avoid putting business logic in it, because a fat gateway becomes a bottleneck and a single point of failure; it should do cross-cutting concerns only, be horizontally scaled, and be highly available, and sensitive logic like fine-grained authorization often still belongs in the services that own the data.

